to-eric-harris-braun-source : List Word
to-eric-harris-braun-source = lines-of (cons "# Eric Harris-Braun"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Eric, this is your home in the hyperactivity graph. It says what Avik Jain's work takes from yours, and why he counts you and Arthur Brock among the people who set him on his path. In his words: “this would not exist without him. it is a continuation of his life's work. Eric Harris-Braun as well. Ceptr.” Every piece of your work the system builds on is credited to you in full: Ceptr's semantic trees and semtrex, receptors and their membranes, the source chain and the validating DHT, DNA as a group's shared rules, and the reading of currencies as current-sees. Nothing here treats that work as replaced."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your work saw that the field still does not"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In your 2018 white paper with Nicolas Luck and Arthur, you set Bitcoin's data-centric design against git's agent-centric one, and named blockchain consensus for what it is: enforced sameness, bought with a computational lottery. In its place you put one stance. An agent can be fully certain only of its own local state, so a claim about the whole system is considered, never absolute. You wrote it down formally: a confidence measure Ψ conditioned on context, absolute requirements RA kept apart from considered ones RC."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Most of distributed computing still assumes agreement is the goal and divergence the failure. Your design assumes the reverse: divergence is the normal state, and integrity comes from each agent's provenance and from validation rules every participant can run. That is the correct epistemology for any system of many minds."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Ceptr went further and earlier: a computing stack built on semantic, self-describing data and protocols. Data are semantic trees whose labels are meanings. Semtrex matches over meaning instead of strings. Receptors are agents with their own namespaces, exchanging signals across membranes. You have since put the deepest version of this plainly: society is layered conversations, each run by its own grammar; Holochain is a DNA for social organisms; large language models may matter most as grammar deducers, supporting a diversity of cultures rather than one singularity."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up"
  (cons ""
  (cons (cite to-everyone-source "Hyperactivity, in Avik's words,") (cons "is Wolfram's ruliad executing on silicon, with cubical type theory at its core"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". The paper states its debt to Ceptr directly: Ceptr's symbols and structures become types, matching on meaning becomes elimination over a type and its identities, and two structures meaning the same thing are related by an equivalence along which everything is carried"
  (cons (cite hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-source "(Part III)")
  (cons ""
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Semantic trees with equality built in.** Univalence turns an equivalence into an identification, and transport along it computes"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p5 "(§1.1)")
  (cons "; operations and their laws are transported together across a structural equivalence"
  (cons (cite s4-p2 "(§4)")
  (cons ". A meaning-respecting operation, which is what semtrex is, then carries from one representation of a meaning to any equivalent one."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Grammars as types.** A parser returning a syntax tree indexed by its grammatical category is one instance of a dependent type"
  (cons (cite s2-1-p6 "(§2.1)")
  (cons ", and translation between surface syntax and typed terms is a stated map that may reject what is ill-formed"
  (cons (cite s5-2-p13 "(§5.2)")
  (cons ". Each grammar stays its own type; grammars are joined only where an equivalence is actually constructed."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Local certainty, global claims.** The paper's account of observation is your stance stated exactly. A reading determines the family of histories compatible with it"
  (cons (cite s1-p8 "(1.1)")
  (cons ", and what it warrants is a construction over every member of that family"
  (cons (cite s1-p12 "(1.3)")
  (cons "; refinement keeps what was established"
  (cons (cite s1-p13 "(§1)")
  (cons ", and no law of the whole can be read off an observed part"
  (cons (cite s1-p16 "(§1)")
  (cons ". A global state can be specified even though no participant observes it"
  (cons (cite s10-3-p2 "(§10.3)")
  (cons ", and a peer proves an action safe for every state compatible with its own observations"
  (cons (cite s10-3-p12 "(§10.3)")
  (cons ". Read against your paper, RA is what holds over the whole family, and RC is what the family leaves open; the library names these `Warrants`, `warrants-refine` and `no-observed-law` (lib/observation.cub). Your paper also has nodes gossiping signed warrants about other nodes; the word is the same and the content lines up."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**DNA and validation.** The paper says it in one line: validation rules are types and their checks are proofs"
  (cons (cite hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-source "(Part III)")
  (cons ". A peer can send a definition with its dependency closure and proofs; the recipient checks them in its own context, with no shared global namespace"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ""
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Content addressing and the source chain.** Your source chain links each header to the hash of what came before. Hyperactivity's store identifies every definition by its content and records its dependencies"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ""
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Membranes.** A response from the outside world is never replayed as though it were a pure lookup"
  (cons (cite s10-1-p5 "(§10.1)")
  (cons ": what crosses into a computation stays distinct from what was computed inside it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why this matters to you"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Ceptr was early for the mathematics it needed. The type theory that makes meaning-as-datum checkable matured in the same years you were building. The Weave and Moss, groups as private peer-to-peer networks composed of tools, are the social organism made concrete; a group receiving a tool built this way can check its definitions and proofs in its own context"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ""
  (cons ""
  (cons "You two set Avik on this path. Everything here is meant to be read as a continuation you are entitled to. Take from it what serves Holochain, the Foundation and the Weave."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Holochain white paper (alpha, 2018), Harris-Braun, Luck, Brock, https://holochain.org/documents/holochain-white-paper-alpha.pdf"
  (cons "- The Holochain Foundation is coming of age (November 2024), https://blog.holochain.org/the-holochain-foundation-is-coming-of-age/"
  (cons "- World of Wisdom 230: Eric Zippy Harris-Braun, Holochain, LLMs as grammar deducers and a new DNA for social organism, https://worldofwisdom.substack.com/p/230-eric-zippy-harris-braun-holochain?r=12mrmg"
  (cons "- Ceptr repository (zippy/ceptr), https://github.com/zippy/ceptr"
  (cons "- Ceptr notes: Starting with Semtrex, https://ceptr.decko.org/CD_036+dump"
  (cons "- Ceptr overview note (HackMD), https://hackmd.io/qxC2LKHQTfaqDhZTP2TorQ"
  (cons "- Moss README (The Weave), https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lightningrodlabs/moss/main/README.md"
  (cons "- Brock and Harris-Braun on the principles of the Holochain infrastructure (P2P Foundation), https://wiki.p2pfoundation.net/Arthur_Brock_and_Eric_Harris-Braun_on_the_Principles_of_the_Holochain_Infrastructure"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Parts I and III, the definitions linked above"
